# Auth-Aware Visual Verification

This project uses Clerk. Agent Playwright sessions start unsigned-in, so protected routes redirect or block before UI can be inspected.

## Do Not Use Playwright For Protected UI

- Do not use Playwright (or browser automation) to visually verify work on auth-required pages unless the user explicitly provides a signed-in session, storage state, or asks to debug auth.
- Do not retry Playwright navigation on protected routes hoping access will appear.

Protected routes (see `src/middleware.ts`):

- `/dashboard` and nested paths
- `/assets/[symbol]` (asset detail; there is no standalone `/assets` browse page)
- `/alerts`
- `/settings`

## Allowed Playwright Use

- Public pages: marketing home (`/`), Clerk sign-in (`/sign-in`), and sign-up (`/sign-up`) when a quick visual check helps.
- Explicit user request to debug auth, sign-in, or session setup with provided credentials or state.

## How To Verify Protected UI Instead

Prefer:

- Code review of components, layouts, and data loaders
- `npm run lint` and `npm run typecheck` (or project equivalents)
- Component-level reasoning against Tripwire UI rules (scores, stale/null badges, labels)

In the final response for protected UI work, state clearly that live visual inspection of protected routes was skipped because Clerk blocks unsigned agent sessions.

## When Visual Verification Is Essential

Ask the user to either:

- Inspect the change in their signed-in browser, or
- Provide a Playwright storage state / session path or other setup the agent can reuse
